    "turn_text": "Alright, welcome back to the show. Good morning. Thanks so much for listening. Hope you were having a fantastic day. Hope you had a great weekend, too. I know it was a little cold here in the panhandle. And I did escape the panhandle over the weekend. I went down to Orlando for a little while, and it was... It wasn't warm, but it sure was warmer than it was here. I don't know what happened here. It's like winter came on and it'll fall on. But that's okay, because then it rained all day yesterday while we were there. So, I guess there's that. But I had to share a story with you about what had happened with me while I was there because I'm actually kind of a big fan of Disney Springs because I don't know why. I just like some of the shopping there, even though a lot of it is pretty bougie and is outside of not only just my price range, but like some of the stuff I just I they've got some stores there that are like the ultra 30A, you know. And it's always interesting to watch certain people shop at some of these stores. And it goes back again sometimes, I think, to the responsibility that some people should have. And I posted a little bit of the story online, on Facebook. But I really didn't go into much detail of the story. But I had gone over to Disney Springs because I wanted to go to two specific stores. One is a sock store, which has amazing socks. And I can't find these socks anywhere else. So I had to go there. And honestly, they were not all that expensive considering you were buying them at a Disney property. You would think that, you know, this is going to cost you an arm. It wasn't too bad. That and they're great socks. So, and then there was another store that I wanted to go at just to see if there's anything new that I liked. There wasn't so I didn't spend any money there. So I did some window shopping. And I just happened to go into a couple of stores and one in particular as I was kind of walking around, I noticed a sign that said, you know, hey, you can pay off your purchase and for easy payments, which I'm thinking to myself and it was a clothing store. I'm kind of thinking to myself going, man, if you can't afford the clothing in that particular store, maybe you shouldn't be going in there and trying to buy it and putting it on a clothing payment plan. And I kind of thought that was silly, but I walked around the store a little bit more. And as I was walking past the counter, there was a particular area in the counter where you could go to fill out the application so that you could pay for your purchase in four easy payments. And sure enough, there were people actually doing it. And to me, that's kind of sad. It's kind of sad that people would actually put themselves into that kind of a financial burden just for some clothes that by the way, I wanted to say to the people at the same time, you know, you can go to the mall to this very same store and pay a whole lot less money for it. Just saying. But it was just, it's kind of one of those observations that I made where to me it kind of seemed a little sad that, you know, people would go down to that point. And you know, it's one of these things that You always hear people say, oh, the rich are so cheap. There's a saying that is out there, which is actually kind of true in a lot of ways, because I've seen this in a lot of situations. But keep this phrase in mind. The rich act as if they're poor, and the poor act as if they are rich. And it's one of those things that keep the two in the class that they are in. It's one of those things where people who are poor, and this isn't everybody, but there's certain people that do this, that they've got to be the ultra bougie and they've got to be out there to impress everybody else. And I think social media has actually done a big disservice when it comes to this, especially the wealthy brats that are on social media because they got nothing better to do. So people see this and they're like, oh my God, I've got to have that too. And they go off and they spend way more money on something that they should. And meanwhile, people who are rich go, you know what? I can afford to go do this and do that. I'm going to do that, but do I really need to spend that much money on that? No. And that is one of the big differences between the classes. It's one of these things that I think a lot of people do the harm to themselves. It's like if you can't afford it, you don't need to buy it. Even if it does look all great and it's going to make you look awesome on your Instagram photos or on your TikTok. Earn the money first. And then if you could afford it, then you're good to go. But these are also the same people, by the way, that complain about not being able to pay their college tuition and their college bills that they've got. Well, hey. So things over at CNN are getting a little bit worse. And Chris Wallace right now, he might actually be regretting a little bit his decision to go over to CNN. There's reports now that the former Fox News anchor Chris Wallace, who went over to CNN to be there for the digital platform that they're doing, is reportedly irate over the ouster of CNN President Jeff Zuckger last week and now feels uncertain about his future at the network. Speaking with radar online, a TV insider said Wallace has been second guessing his decision to leave Fox News for the upcoming CNN plus streaming platform in the wake of Zucker's resignation for his undisclosed affair with his subordinate, which I got to talk with you about that in a second. That story is getting bigger. But the report was that Chris is the type of person who makes it known if he doesn't like something. And the source told the outlet that he went over there for Zucker and now Zucker is gone and Wallace feels that he has been stiffed. And he's got no staff, no executive producer. And the guy he gave up a prized gig, he just walked out the door. So apparently now, Chris Wallace, who to be honest with, I don't think is all that welcome back in Fox News. Anyhow, upset second guessing. I mean, Chris, come on, you're a smart guy at all. You go from the number one network to the network that the home shopping network has beat out in the ratings. I mean, really, dude, what did you expect? Come on, let's look at that a little bit of reality, shall we? But more things going on with this fiasco over at CNN and now reportedly CNN is ready to pay Chris Cuomo, the $9 million in hush money, which essentially is the rest of his contract, just to zip him up. So to keep Chrissy Cuomo quiet, a yo, Fredo over here, but they want to keep it quiet about Jeff Zuckers, unethical dealings with Andrew Cuomo. which a little too late now because all of it's all coming out. So at this point, just let it roll, you know, save your $9 million and let it roll because to be honest with you, it can't do much more damage to CNN. I mean, it's lost 90% of its audience. Do you think that the scandal coming out about a guy that is no longer working for the company is going to inherit the company even more? Not really. But after the CNN chief resigned in disgrace last week, It was revealed that Zucker was, um, well, you know, having a little brown chicken, brown cow with his, uh, subordinate, Allison Gullsett, who would also, by the way, served as then governor Cuomo's communications director. Oh, don't, don't, don't. The plot is thickening and CNN's breathtakingly ethical lapse involving the Cuomo brothers suddenly made perfect sense. When you start connecting some of the dots and looking at the timeline when Jeff Zucker took over the reins back in 2013, he immediately hired his little new side piece to come in and what was she like? Something in marketing or whatever. But she had been working for Andrew Cuomo. And then after Allison comes on board, Zucker then hires Andrew Cuomo's little bro, Fredo. And he ended up handing him the anchor chair on the morning show New Day, which was at the time Zucker's big high profile launch. And then after Chris proved to be an absolute ratings disaster in New Day, he still promoted him and put him in prime time. That was smart. And then it then in the run up to the 2020 presidential election, CNN pulls out all the stops to make Andrew president of the United States. CNN ignores and dismisses Andrews scandals. CNN attacks Andrews critics. CNN and Andrew hours of unfiltered airtime to varnish his his image as a national leader. And meanwhile, Chris Cuomo's gob smacking pile of ethics scandals are also just as much ignored. And after CNN was forced to fire Chris back in August, Fredo then threatened to file a lawsuit if he was not paid the rest of his remaining $18 million contract. And now the New York Post has said that Chris is so much dirt, he's ready to let it fly. And CNN is ready to pay him half of the $18 million to keep his mouth shut about Zucker's unethical relationship with Andrew Cuomo. I say let it fly, just let it go. That's what I say. just be done with it. Let, let Fredo go off and do it because it's just going to come off petty. And again, it's not going to affect CNN anymore. I mean, you can't hurt the ratings anymore than it already is. They've already fired Zucker. I mean, maybe with Fredo coming out with more of the juicy gossip about what was going on at CNN, maybe that can lead to more people at CNN being exited, and it'll give AT&T the opportunity to really truly reboot CNN and make it a much better news channel. But I don't know. I guess we'll have to wait and see. But either way, Fredo wanting his $9 million possibly. just to keep it all quiet, but it's all already come out. So that would, in my opinion, be a class of waste of money. Just knock it off. Just let it go. Anyhow, got to take a quick break. We'll be back to second. Don't forget, Congressman Neal Dunn is going to be joining us on the show this morning. He's going to be in studio. We'll have him for a full hour at the seven o'clock hour. A lot of questions are already starting to pour in. If you've got more questions for the Congressman, hit me up on Facebook, facebook.com forward slash the Brian Rush Show or head over to my website, Brian Rush dot us or news talk one on one dot com and send a message. as to what it is that you want to have the question asked to the congressman. We will present him with all the questions that we can. Hang on. You got to take that quick break. We'll be right back. This is the Brian Russ Show. You're listening to News Talk at 101. Good morning.",
