    "turn_text": "All right. Welcome back to the show. Thanks so much for listening coming up next hour. We have Congressman Neil Duns going to be joining us in studio for a full hour. That's going to be a great conversation. I enjoyed the last one that we had. Got a lot of topics to hit. I've also got a lot of questions already sent in by you guys. So we will hit him up with some of those questions too. Also, later on this week, Senator Marco Rubio is going to be joining us on the show on Thursday. Tomorrow we do have the warrior beach retreat people will be in we're gonna talk about the beach retreat which is coming up and how you can help out with that and Then I got a special guest on Wednesday that I'm looking forward to this guy is gonna be interesting He he's got a book out and I can't even really say the name of the book because well, it's a It's got some things that I would say in a normal basis, but his name is Jeremy Murphy and he's going to be on to talk with us about his new book and kind of the princesses in media. You're going to enjoy that one. I promise you on that. But the New York Times is now out warning something that I've said it a few times. You've always got to watch out for that thing called precedent. And the New York Times now report as a warning that Republicans can now use the January 6th Committee's precedent to pursue Democrats after the midterm elections. Ooh, that's not going to be good, guys. Told you you need to be careful because what goes around comes around. But the New York Times warned on Saturday the Democrats are setting a dangerous precedent when the January 6 committee exceeding the constitutional powers of Congress in a way that Republicans can use when they take back the majority. The Times, Michael Schmidt, who also has a Pulitzer Prize, reported that the committee is borrowing techniques. from federal prosecutions employing aggressive tactics typically used against monsters and terrorists. But then again, I mean all Republicans and anybody that supported Donald Trump is a bunch of domestic terrorists, that and parents, parents who stand up for their children. Those guys, domestic terrorists too. But they continue to say that they use it to develop evidence that could prompt a criminal case against former President Donald Trump, even if, even though it has no authority to pursue criminal charges. The committee is kind of hoping that the hope to push Attorney General, Mayor Garland, to pursue charges against Trump and his associates, ignoring the fact that President Joe Biden Repeatedly pledged that the Department of Justice under Biden administration would not be used as a political weapon, and yet, here we are, loading up the ammo. But the Times did warn that the Republicans could use the same tactics against the Democrats if they win the 2022 midterms. And Schmidt had said that having lived through and being part of every major congressional investigation over the past 50 years from Iran, contrary to whitewater to everything else, this is the mother of all investigations and a quantum leap for Congress in the way I've never seen before. That actually came from a Stanley Bran who is a Democrat and former top lawyer for the house who is now representing Dan Scavino, one of Mr. Trump's closest aides in the investigation. Mr. Brandt said it's a development that Democrats might one day come to regret. When a frontier is pushing back, is pushed back, it doesn't recede. And he said that they think they're fighting for the survival of democracy and the ends justify the means, just wait for the Republicans to take over. And that is the thing that the Democrats are on this, this, this kick that they're trying to save democracy. But what's amazing is when you, if you know democracy and you know the basic meaning of democracy, which in a nutshell is the people get to tell the government not the other way around. And of course, the United States of America is not a democracy, even though we do practice democracy, we are a constitutional republic. I don't think it's confusing sometimes, especially when the Democrats are always saying, we got to save our democracy. But when you look at all the policies that the Democrats have been pushing for the past few years, like the voting rights, where they're gonna take away the power of all of the local superintendents of election, and all of the states would lose their power when it comes to elections, and it would all be brought under one umbrella underneath the federal government, because I mean, hey, if that doesn't scream democracy, nothing does. For the federal government to control all the elections, come on. That's not endangering democracy at all. That is strengthening it, right? And then you look at all the other socialist plans and programs that the Democrats actually want to have, that is not democracy. And yet the Democrats cry over and over and over again in Washington about how big bad Donald Trump is a threat to our democracy. And that all these parents that are out actually talking about how they should be in charge of their own children's education, they're a threat to our democracy. I love the Democrats' use of words. because they always try to come across sounding like they're super smart because remember, and you can ask most of them. They're smarter than you because they went to college and they read books like we don't, but whatever. Sometimes it's hard to argue with stupid. I mean, you can duct tape until it stops moving, but sometimes when it comes to arguing with it, that's usually an uphill battle. But the Times also described a Republican Liz Cheney as an instigating force. saying that some of the Democrats on the committee were concerned that if the panel was too aggressive, Republicans might turn the tables on the Democrats whenever they took back control of the House. But Ms. Cheney insisted that the committee be as aggressive as possible. Because Liz Cheney is just so butthurt over everything with Donald Trump that she won't let go. And of course over the weekend, both Liz Cheney and what's his name there, the Weiner. forgot his name Kinzinger. They both got censured by the GOP. The entire party is sick of both of them. It's like, get out both of you. But the Times also noted that the committee is using subpoenas to collect personal bank information and phone records of a lot of people who have no clear connection to the January 6th Capitol riot, including attorneys for the president and his campaign. But don't worry about it because the Democrats are here to protect your democracy. They want to get all this personal information away from people who don't have it. Yeah, come on, knock it off. But in addition, the paper knows that Mr. Trump's allies have grown angry, not just at the aggressiveness of the committee. For example, in making subpoenas public before they have even been served, but also at the expansive list of people questioned, some of whom these allies maintain had minimum to no involvement in the events of January 6th. And I agree this is going to be bad for the Democrats when the Republicans take charge. I mean, we've already heard from Kevin McCarthy and a few others that they're going to impeach President Biden. And when you stop and look at all the other things that have happened with some of the Democrats, like Nancy Pelosi as a perfect example, she's going to lose the speaker of the house. She's not going to have the ability to block anything. And they're going to come after her for her insider trading. They're going to come after her for all of the involvement that she had with the Chinese government. And there's others that they're going to do that same thing. And I said it from the beginning that this whole select committee on January 6 was going to be a disaster. And I underestimated how much of a disaster is going to be because now I think it's all setting up to backfire on the Democrats. I agree with the New York Times 100% on that. this is not going to be good for them. But I think the Democrats are also in this mindset that they were going to be able to pass the voting rights legislations, legislations, and that they were going to be able to control the elections and no Democrat or the Republicans will never be able to take control again. But that has failed on them and it has been exposed big time on them. So what are they going to do now? And I think this is another reason for why you have 28 different Democrats that are like, nope, hanging that up. Even though some of them are actually running for other offices, there's still a whole lot of them that are saying, deuces, we're out. And to be honest with you, I wouldn't be surprised if there's not a few more that'll also be jumping off that ship because that ship is taking on water like you wouldn't believe. And as much as I want to say that the Republicans when they do take charge this November and they get sworn in in January, I want to say don't do it, but at the same time, you know what? Do it. Go after him. And teach him a lesson that this is what you get when you go down that road. And the thing is with this investigation, they're making things up. They're ignoring evidence that, you know, is public, let alone the evidence that they've already got. And you've got people like Liz Cheney, and you've got Adam Kinzinger who just despised Trump, and no matter what happens, they're gonna get him. And then you've got the same thing on the Democrat side, like Pencil Next Shift. That guy, I mean, I don't know if Donald Trump slept with one of his girlfriends or something or what it is, but man, he has got it out for Donald Trump in the absolute worst way. He really, truly does. But it's going to be interesting to see what happens. And this is why sometimes you've got to make sure that you keep things within the realm of your ability and your constitutional rights and responsibilities. But the Democrats, they're going all out. And I think this is going to be a major, major backfire for them in the end. But we'll wait and see. We'll have to see what exactly happens. Uh, but coming up in the next hour, we do have a Congressman Neil Dunn is going to be joining us. He'll be in the studio. We will talk with him in a little bit. In the meantime, two, three, three, nine, eight, it's a never good morning. Welcome to the Brian Rush show.",
